sections=[
('ONE TINY JOB',"A green light says your AI is running. Great. But can it actually do anything? Today we're checking one tiny job: answer a question. First, we check whether the app responds. Then, whether it can reach the model. Finally, whether the answer makes sense. Because an app can be awake and still have absolutely nothing going on upstairs. One job. One result. Let's see what actually works."),
('START WITH WHAT HAPPENED',"Here is what happened on this computer. We checked the local connection listed in Nori's settings. It did not respond. We tried two read-only checks, and neither connected. That tells us where to start. It does not tell us that a model is missing, or that the whole project is broken. We have a connection problem to investigate first. Imagine calling a restaurant and getting no answer. You have not learned whether the chef can cook. You have learned that nobody picked up. Ordering a twelve-course dinner at that point would be ambitious. So we stop there and check the connection. The video shows the actual result of those checks. No successful AI answer is being claimed."),
('THREE QUESTIONS, THREE DIFFERENT ANSWERS',"Now let's make the difference easy to see. These next examples explain the checks; they are not a recording of a successful Nori conversation. Question one: does the app answer at all? A response means we have reached something. Question two: can it use the model it needs? Think of the model as the part that works out the answer. The app can answer the door while that part is unavailable. Question three: did it do the actual job? For our tiny example, ask what two plus two equals. The expected answer is four. An answer of five fails the task, even if every status light is green. These are three different results. Reached the app. Reached the model. Got the right answer. Keep them separate so a green light cannot talk you into ignoring a wrong answer."),
('MAKE FAILURE USEFUL',"There is a small example of this in Nori's worker code. One check reports that the worker is listening. Another checks whether its model is available. The code can report that it is not ready. That is useful information. It still needs to lead to a useful action. If a model is unavailable, explain that and let the user know what to fix. If the connection is down, say that. If an answer is wrong, show the wrong answer and the expected result. A message that says something went wrong is about as helpful as a mechanic pointing at your car and saying, yep, car. Tell people which part failed. Then repeat that small test after the fix. You do not need a giant demonstration to find the first problem."),
('THE RESULT IS THE POINT',"That is the approach for this Nori build. Give it one small job, show what happened, and fix the first thing that stops the job. Today's actual check stopped at the connection. The next successful demonstration needs a responding service and a real answer before we call that task working. A diagram can help explain the route, but the result is what matters. When the simple task works, try a second one. When something fails, keep that failure in the story. Watching a project go wrong and get fixed is more useful than watching a slideshow declare victory. What is one small task you would want Nori to handle first? Put it in the comments. Subscribe to follow the build, including the bits that refuse to cooperate.")]
narration='\n\n'.join(t for _,t in sections)+'\n'
